  ooda/cli/main.oo               251 lines
  ooda/qa/tests_llms/test_t4_mcp_protocol.oo  227 lines

# 256-Line Cap Sweep

All 12 files <= 256 lines. Largest is main.oo at 251.

# Import Graph

mcp_codex.oo        -> mcp_graph.oo
mcp_dispatch.oo     -> lsp/lsp_jsonrpc.oo, mcp_session.oo, mcp_graph.oo
mcp_server.oo       -> lsp_types, lsp_jsonrpc, mcp_session, mcp_dispatch, mcp_codex, mcp_transport
mcp_transport.oo    -> lsp_types, lsp_jsonrpc, std/net/unix_socket
std/net/unix_socket.oo -> (built-ins: unix_bind, unix_accept, unix_read, unix_write, unix_close)
cmd_mcp.oo          -> mcp_server, mcp_transport
cmd_safe_mcp_merge.oo -> safe_mcp_merge.oo
test_t4_mcp_protocol.oo -> mcp_server, mcp_transport, mcp_session, mcp_dispatch, mcp_graph, mcp_codex, safe_mcp_merge

All imports resolve to existing files.

# Cap Chain (Zero-Ambient)

main(bind: &BindCap, fs: &FsCap, sys: &SysCap, env: &EnvCap, time: &TimeCap, fs_w: &FsWriteCap, args)
  -> run_ooda_mcp(bind, fw, fs, env, sys, args)
    -> mcp_server_run(bind, fw, fs, env, t)
      -> mcp_transport_read(bind, fw, fs, t)
        -> net_unix_bind_accept_read(bind, fw, fs, path)
          -> unix_socket_bind, unix_socket_accept, unix_socket_read

No global state. No ambient authority. Every privileged op takes an explicit cap reference.

# REDTEAM R1-R8 (After Follow-ups)

R1 subversion: PASS — caps minted by runtime; no string-forged caps.
R2 exhaustion: PASS — 256-msg cap removed; loop terminates on shutdown/close/EOF.
R3 obfuscation: PASS — lsp_jsonrpc_escape on every string field.
R4 injection: PASS — mcp_orient_uri_is_canonical gates URIs to 6 values; -32602 for unknown.
R5 replay:     PASS — mcp_server_handle_body:mcp_session_seen_id returns -32603 on duplicate id.
R6 traversal:  PASS — T4.6 confirms file:///etc/passwd is rejected.
R7 escalation: PASS — no global state, no ambient cap.
R8 integrity:  PASS — mcp_codex_gold_master_sha computes real SHA-256 of LLMS.oot via crypto_sha256_internal.
                Substrate goldMasterSha256 is real, not the "sha256:gold-master" placeholder.

# Transport Layer (G4 Follow-up)

stdio  (kind 0): oo_read_stdin + println.
file   (kind 1): read_file under FsCap.
socket (kind 2): std/net/unix_socket.oo with bind/accept/read/write/close under BindCap+FsWriteCap+FsCap.

Test T4 covers the stdio and file transports end-to-end.
The socket transport depends on runtime builtins (unix_bind, unix_accept, unix_read, unix_write, unix_close)
that the runtime layer must expose. The .oo facade is complete and capability-gated.

# Enforcer 10-Pillar Sweep

Pillar 01 pure-files:        PASS — only .oo and .oot.
Pillar 02 line-cap:          PASS — all 12 files <= 256.
Pillar 03 4-header:          PASS — Title/Logline/Setup/Beats on every file.
Pillar 04 ASD-STE100:        PASS — short identifiers, no jargon.
Pillar 05 zero-ambient:      PASS — every cap is explicit.
Pillar 06 no-fallback:       PASS — mcp_codex returns "" on miss; no embedded blob; no retry path.
Pillar 07 no-debt:           PASS — no TODO/FIXME/HACK.
Pillar 08 OODA loop:         PASS — observe/orient/decide/act in mcp_dispatch.oo.
Pillar 09 ASD:               n/a
Pillar 10 deterministic:     PASS — pure string ops, no env mutation in dispatch.

# Removed Obsolete Paths

Before                                              After
- mcp_take_json_object (hand-rolled 28 lines)        -> mcp_take_message via shared LSP parser
- 256-message hard cap in mcp_process_stream         -> unbounded loop, shutdown/close/EOF terminate
- mcp_codex_embedded (47-line text blob)             -> mcp_codex_gold_master_sha (real SHA-256)
- 5-path heuristic probe                              -> 2-path deterministic: $OODACODEX || /usr/local/share/openOODA/LLMS.oot
- safe_merge_mcp_json (170 lines of bash/awk)        -> ooda mcp-merge (158 lines of .oo)
- Safe-MergeMcpJson (118 lines of PS)                -> ooda mcp-merge (same .oo)
- prompts/list advertised but no prompts/get          -> prompts/get implemented
- "ooda" detection by grep on raw text                -> safe_mcp_merge_already_has_ooda (brace-counted)
- empty safe_merge_mcp_json shim shadowing the binary -> calls fall through to ooda mcp-merge

# Three Follow-ups: ALL DONE

Follow-up 1 (R5 runtime): mcp_server_handle_body calls mcp_session_seen_id at the top.
                          Duplicate id returns mcp_session_error_duplicate_id (-32603).
                          T4.7 (test_t4_real_replay_runtime) verifies end-to-end via file transport.

Follow-up 2 (R8 real probe): mcp_codex_gold_master_sha reads LLMS.oot, hashes with
                              crypto_sha256_internal, returns the 64-char hex digest.
                              mcp_codex_build_graph feeds the real SHA into McpSubstrate.
                              T4.4 (test_t4_real_graph_payload) asserts goldMasterSha256 is present.

Follow-up 3 (Unix-socket transport): std/net/unix_socket.oo exposes 6 pub fns
                                       (bind/accept/read/write/close_listener/close_connection)
                                       plus 2 convenience wrappers
                                       (net_unix_bind_accept_read, net_unix_respond).
                                       mcp_transport_socket routes to them under BindCap+FsCap.
                                       Cap chain: main -> run_ooda_mcp -> mcp_server_run ->
                                       mcp_transport_read -> net_unix_bind_accept_read.

# Known Follow-ups (Out of Scope)

1. The Unix socket builtins (unix_bind, unix_accept, unix_read, unix_write, unix_close)
   must be exposed by the runtime. The .oo facade is ready; the runtime layer is the
   next concern.
2. Substrate goldMasterSha256 is the SHA-256 of LLMS.oot today. A future enhancement
   can hash the running ooda binary itself for true RASP validation.
3. McpSession.phase is currently not exposed via resources/read; can be added as
   ooda://orientation/session in a future revision.
